1
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:36,780
Chromatography. The purification of chemicals is one of the most important

2
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:41,460
jobs of a research chemist. Perhaps the most common purification technique in

3
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:46,320
an organic laboratory is column chromatography. This technique takes

4
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:50,740
advantage of the different polarities of different compounds to separate mixtures

5
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:56,060
frequently on the gram scale. Technically, column chromatography can be

6
00:00:56,060 --> 00:01:00,180
challenging, but with a little practice you will be able to separate mixtures

7
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:08,820
of compounds consistently and successfully. As the name suggests, a

8
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:14,740
column of adsorbent, either silica or alumina, is packed into a glass tube with

9
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:19,380
a stopcock at the bottom. The sample is applied to the top of the adsorbent and

10
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:23,700
solvent is run through the column until the compound is flushed out of the

11
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:30,850
bottom. In most cases, flash column chromatography is used, meaning that

12
00:01:30,850 --> 00:01:35,170
pressure is applied to the top of the column resulting in faster running times

13
00:01:35,490 --> 00:01:41,780
and better separation. Before you can run a column, you need to decide on the

14
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:46,260
appropriate conditions. It is vital that you pick a solvent system that will

15
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:51,780
provide good separation. In general, you will use a mixture of two miscible

16
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:58,660
solvents, one polar and one nonpolar. Luckily, TLC is an effective tool for

17
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:05,680
deciding on the optimal solvent system for your sample. Make a TLC sample by

18
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:09,760
dissolving a small amount of your material in approximately one milliliter

19
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:15,440
of solvent. Use this sample to spot several TLC plates and develop each

20
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:19,600
plate in a different solvent system. Make sure that the adsorbent on your

21
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:23,840
TLC plates is the same as the adsorbent you will use in the column,

22
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:30,720
either silica or alumina. Most commonly, you will use a solvent system

23
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:37,280
composed of a mixture of epoacetate and hexane. But when you are purifying

24
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:42,240
a volatile compound, it is a good idea to use a lower boiling solvent system,

25
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:50,030
such as a mixture of ether and pentane. Mixtures of methanol and dichloromethane

26
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:56,430
are frequently used to purify highly polar compounds. When you are removing

27
00:02:56,430 --> 00:03:01,230
small amounts of impurities from a sample, focus on the major constituent.

28
00:03:03,810 --> 00:03:08,450
Once you have found a solvent system that separates the components of your sample,

29
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:13,330
adjust the polarity so that the major, and hopefully the desired constituent,

30
00:03:13,330 --> 00:03:22,510
has an RF of approximately 0.3. If the RF is too low, then the solvent system is too

31
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:27,310
nonpolar, and it will take a very long time for the material to come off of the column.

32
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:34,860
Alternatively, if the RF is too high, then the solvent system is too polar,

33
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:39,180
and the material will come off of the column very quickly with poor separation.

34
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:46,460
When you are separating a mixture of two or more compounds, adjust the solvent polarity,

35
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:51,820
such that the midpoint between the spots is at an RF of approximately 0.3.

36
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:58,260
When you are separating two or more compounds with very different polarity,

37
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:03,460
it will save you time to increase the polarity of the solvent as the column proceeds.

38
00:04:05,630 --> 00:04:09,870
Begin the column with a solvent system that puts the least polar compound

39
00:04:09,950 --> 00:04:16,910
at an RF of approximately 0.3. Once that compound has completely come off of the column,

40
00:04:16,910 --> 00:04:24,430
slowly increase the polarity of the solvent to a mixture that puts the next spot at an RF of 0.3.

41
00:04:25,710 --> 00:04:29,710
Continue this until all desired spots have come off of the column.

42
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:37,250
Once you have picked an appropriate solvent system, you need to decide how much adsorbent to use.

43
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:41,810
This video will illustrate the use of a silica gel column,

44
00:04:41,810 --> 00:04:44,690
but the same procedure can be followed using alumina.

45
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:55,140
Caution. Silica and alumina are highly toxic when inhaled. Handle adsorbents in the hood.

46
00:04:57,410 --> 00:05:04,610
In cases with good separation, a 20 to 1 mass ratio of silica gel to compound is usually

47
00:05:04,610 --> 00:05:10,290
adequate. This means that for 1 gram of compound, you would use 20 grams of silica.

48
00:05:10,290 --> 00:05:18,050
It is good to use as little adsorbent as possible, but when the separation is more difficult,

49
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:24,210
ratios of 50 or 100 to 1 may be necessary. Choosing the appropriate amount of adsorbent

50
00:05:24,210 --> 00:05:29,730
takes practice. Until you have developed your own intuition, it is a good idea to ask a more

51
00:05:29,730 --> 00:05:36,450
experienced chemist for advice. Weigh the silica into an Erlenmeyer flask in the hood.

52
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:41,090
Use a flask large enough that you don't fill it more than 1 third full.

53
00:05:43,010 --> 00:05:47,010
The last major decision to make is what diameter of column to use.

54
00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:52,610
Different people have very different opinions about this, but in general, it is a good idea

55
00:05:52,610 --> 00:05:57,810
to choose a column that will fill 6 to 7 inches with the chosen amount of silica.

56
00:06:00,590 --> 00:06:05,390
In a taller column, the different bands of compounds will disperse and overlap,

57
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:14,480
but a shorter column will not have enough surface area to give good separation.

58
00:06:17,330 --> 00:06:20,610
Packing a column with adsorbent can be fairly tricky,

59
00:06:20,610 --> 00:06:23,890
and it takes practice to be able to do it well every time.

60
00:06:25,650 --> 00:06:30,210
The first step involves plugging the bottom of the column with a small piece of cotton

61
00:06:30,210 --> 00:06:35,570
to prevent loss of silica gel. Roll a small piece of cotton between your fingers

62
00:06:35,570 --> 00:06:41,970
and drop it into the column. Tilt and tap the column until the cotton settles into

63
00:06:41,970 --> 00:06:48,530
the center depression. Using a long stick, gently pack the cotton into the hole. Don't

64
00:06:48,530 --> 00:06:53,010
pack the cotton too tightly or it will be difficult to force solvent through the plug.

65
00:06:54,770 --> 00:06:58,450
To continue packing the column, you will need an empty beaker,

66
00:06:59,090 --> 00:07:02,930
a pipette, a supply of your chosen solvent mixture,

67
00:07:03,570 --> 00:07:10,770
a funnel, your pre-weighed silica gel, some sand, and a flash pressure apparatus.

68
00:07:12,850 --> 00:07:17,650
With the cotton in place, clamp the column in a straight vertical position

69
00:07:17,650 --> 00:07:23,490
and use a funnel to pour in a small layer of sand, approximately 1 to 2 millimeters.

70
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:30,610
Now, fill the column with approximately 5 inches of solvent mixture. Make sure that

71
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:39,360
the sand layer is flat and no sand is stuck to the sides. Pour enough of the solvent mixture

72
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:50,560
into the silica gel to form a mobile slurry. Swirl the silica and carefully pour it into

73
00:07:50,560 --> 00:08:04,190
the column, making sure that you do not disturb the sand layer. Use additional solvent to add

74
00:08:04,190 --> 00:08:14,800
the remaining silica. Once you have added all of the silica, rinse the funnel and sides of the

75
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:24,610
column with a small amount of solvent. Apply pressure to the top of the column and open

76
00:08:24,610 --> 00:08:30,450
the stopcock, tapping the sides of the column gently to make sure that the silica layer is flat.

77
00:08:32,210 --> 00:08:36,770
Drain the solvent until 2 or 3 inches remain above the silica layer.

78
00:08:36,770 --> 00:08:41,330
Then close the stopcock and remove the pressure.

79
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:48,780
Caution! Make sure there are no bubbles before you add sand.

80
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:57,120
Bubbles and inconsistencies in your column will cause serious problems later.

81
00:08:58,640 --> 00:09:05,120
To avoid bubbles, use a very wet slurry of silica gel, gently tap the sides of the

82
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:11,120
column while packing the silica, and apply pressure before opening the stopcock.

83
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:20,400
When you have achieved a uniform layer of adsorbent, use a pipette to rinse any excess

84
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:28,300
silica down from the sides of the column. Using the funnel, carefully add a small layer of sand.

85
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:38,700
Be careful not to disturb the top of the silica gel. Use a small amount of solvent

86
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:48,430
to wash the excess sand from the sides of the column. Apply pressure, open the stopcock,

87
00:09:50,270 --> 00:09:53,470
and drain the solvent to just above the silica gel.

88
00:10:04,590 --> 00:10:09,710
Caution! Never let the solvent layer drop below the top of the column.

89
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:17,300
Now you're ready to load your sample onto the column. There are a couple of ways to do this.

90
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,760
If your sample is soluble in the chosen solvent system,

91
00:10:23,760 --> 00:10:26,800
then it is easiest to load your sample as a solution.

92
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:33,520
Dissolve your sample in the smallest possible volume of the chosen solvent mixture.

93
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:38,560
If you have trouble getting it to dissolve, try adding a few drops of methylene chloride.

94
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:43,600
It is vital that your sample is completely dissolved before you add it to the column.

95
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:50,980
Slowly drip the solution of sample around the edges of the column,

96
00:10:50,980 --> 00:10:57,380
being careful not to disturb the sand layer. Lower the solvent level to just above the silica,

97
00:10:58,260 --> 00:11:03,540
rinse the flask with a small amount of solvent, and add the rinse in the same fashion.

98
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:15,520
Lower the solvent level again and repeat the rinse one or two times.

99
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:32,800
It is a good idea to rinse any residual sample from the sides of the column,

100
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:35,600
lowering the solvent level in between each rinse.

101
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:45,780
Now you're ready to fill the column with solvent.

102
00:11:46,580 --> 00:11:53,780
Start by slowly adding solvent with a pipette. Once you have added a nice cushion, slowly pour the

103
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:59,380
remaining solvent into the column, being careful not to disturb the sand or the silica.

104
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:06,260
Done correctly, you should end up with a thin band of sample just below the sand.

105
00:12:06,260 --> 00:12:13,780
When your sample is largely insoluble in the chosen solvent mixture, it is more effective

106
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:19,540
to pre-absorb the sample onto a small quantity of silica and add it as a dry mixture.

107
00:12:20,340 --> 00:12:26,340
To do this, you will need some methylene chloride, a round bottom flask, and some silica gel.

108
00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:34,220
Start by dissolving your sample in methylene chloride and transferring it to the round

109
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:43,630
bottom flask. Make sure you complete the transfer with a few rinses,

110
00:12:44,190 --> 00:12:56,460
then add a small amount of silica gel to the flask and swirl to mix.

111
00:12:58,460 --> 00:13:04,460
Before you can remove the solvent on the rotavap, stuff a few kim wipes into the bump trap

112
00:13:04,460 --> 00:13:09,340
to prevent contamination of the rotavap with the very fine silica powder.

113
00:13:19,140 --> 00:13:24,020
Attach your flask to the bump trap and concentrate it on the rotavap until you

114
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:31,410
obtain a fine, free-flowing powder. If the mixture remains clumpy or sticky,

115
00:13:31,410 --> 00:13:35,090
you may need to re-dissolve the sample and add more silica gel.

116
00:13:37,330 --> 00:13:40,930
Don't add it to your column until it is a free-flowing powder.

117
00:13:43,460 --> 00:13:49,380
To dry-load your sample, pack the column as before but leave a small layer of solvent

118
00:13:49,380 --> 00:14:03,200
above the sand. Pour the pre-absorbed silica onto the top of the sand. Use a small amount

119
00:14:03,280 --> 00:14:09,040
of the solvent mixture to rinse the flask and wash the silica down from the sides of the column.

120
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:24,510
Drain the solvent to the top of the silica and add a small layer of sand. Carefully fill the

121
00:14:24,510 --> 00:14:33,620
column with solvent as before. One alternative to dry-loading is loading your sample as a solution

122
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:38,100
in a solvent that is more polar than the solvent system you're using for your column.

123
00:14:38,100 --> 00:14:43,380
You will end up with a thick band of compound and poor separation.

124
00:14:45,380 --> 00:14:49,780
Once your sample is loaded on the column, you must begin running it immediately,

125
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:53,780
and it's a good idea not to take any breaks until it's through.

126
00:14:55,620 --> 00:15:00,820
Before you start, make sure you have an adequate supply of your chosen solvent mixture

127
00:15:00,820 --> 00:15:06,980
and a nice big rack of test tubes. Begin running the column by placing a test

128
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:18,270
tube at the bottom of the column, applying pressure to the top, and opening the stopcock.

129
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:25,520
If the pressure is right, you should have a fairly rapid flow of solvent out of the bottom of the

130
00:15:25,520 --> 00:15:35,730
column. You may have to adjust the pressure so that the flow rate isn't a slow drip or

131
00:15:36,450 --> 00:15:52,370
an out-of-control stream, but something right in the middle. Don't forget to continually collect

132
00:15:52,370 --> 00:16:05,950
the eluent in test tubes. You have to pay attention so that the solvent doesn't overflow.

133
00:16:21,410 --> 00:16:26,610
Caution. Pay close attention to the solvent level and refill frequently.

134
00:16:31,150 --> 00:16:34,750
Now your hard work is over and it's time to see if it paid off.

135
00:16:36,110 --> 00:16:40,030
When your compound is colored, it can be easy to find it in the fractions,

136
00:16:40,830 --> 00:16:44,830
but a more reliable method for monitoring your column is with TLC.

137
00:16:45,310 --> 00:16:51,390
You can easily spot five or six fractions per TLC plate. Make sure you rinse your spotter in

138
00:16:51,390 --> 00:16:57,390
between fractions. Develop the plates with a solvent mixture that spreads the spots out

139
00:16:57,390 --> 00:17:02,350
on the plate. It does not have to be the same solvent system you use for the column.

140
00:17:02,910 --> 00:17:09,440
If it goes well, you will end up with a maximum of one compound per fraction,

141
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:14,080
with a few clean fractions in between the compounds you're trying to separate.

142
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:19,920
Unfortunately, it doesn't always work out so well.

143
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:27,440
Overlapping fractions containing more than one compound are the sign of a failed separation.

144
00:17:29,280 --> 00:17:35,840
So what went wrong? If all of the material elutes in just a few fractions,

145
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:44,000
then the column may have been too small or the developing solvent too polar. On the other hand,

146
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:49,680
if the compounds are spread out over many fractions, then the column may have been too

147
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:59,490
tall or you used too much solvent to load your sample. Once the last compound has come off of

148
00:17:59,490 --> 00:18:05,330
the column and you have analyzed your TLC plates, it is time to combine the desired fractions.

149
00:18:07,330 --> 00:18:12,770
Combine all of the fractions containing the same pure compound in a round bottom flask.

150
00:18:15,200 --> 00:18:20,400
Fractions containing more than one compound should be set aside for further purification.

151
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:28,240
It is a good idea to rinse the test tubes two or three times with a little bit of clean solvent

152
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:35,570
and add those rinses to the flask as well. Don't fill the flask more than half full.

153
00:18:35,570 --> 00:18:38,450
Remember, you need to concentrate it on the rotavap.

154
00:18:40,210 --> 00:18:45,490
Removing solvent from your compound is the last step in a chromatographic purification.

155
00:18:49,900 --> 00:18:54,300
Never discard any fractions until you recover the desired material.

156
00:18:56,220 --> 00:19:01,580
It is a good idea to clean up the silica before you discard it in the appropriate waste container.

157
00:19:02,780 --> 00:19:06,300
When you are sure that there is no more desired material on the column,

158
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:10,940
fill the column with ethanol, place a flask underneath,

159
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:15,260
and push air through the column until the silica is dry and free flowing.

160
00:19:15,260 --> 00:19:22,700
At this point, it will be easy to discard the silica by pouring it into the proper waste container.

161
00:19:25,700 --> 00:19:28,740
When you need to purify a small amount of compound,

162
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:32,420
it is sometimes easier to run a micro column in a pipette.

163
00:19:34,260 --> 00:19:39,460
On small scale, the packing and sample loading steps are greatly simplified.

164
00:19:41,300 --> 00:19:46,180
It is easiest to pack a micro column completely dry without any solvent.

165
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:50,580
Plug the end of the pipette with a small piece of cotton.

166
00:19:51,380 --> 00:19:55,540
Then, add a couple of inches of adsorbent and a thin layer of sand.

167
00:19:57,620 --> 00:20:02,100
Next, add your sample, pre-absorbed onto a small amount of adsorbent.

168
00:20:03,220 --> 00:20:06,340
Top it off with a thin layer of sand and you are ready to go.

169
00:20:09,170 --> 00:20:14,610
To run the column, place a collection flask or a test tube underneath the pipette,

170
00:20:15,410 --> 00:20:19,010
and use another pipette to add solvent to the top of the column.

171
00:20:21,680 --> 00:20:25,200
Keep adding solvent until your compound has come off of the column.

172
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,160
Make sure you don't let the column run dry.

173
00:20:28,720 --> 00:20:57,220
This video has outlined the basic steps that you will use to perform a purification

174
00:20:57,220 --> 00:21:02,340
using column chromatography. Now it's time to get in the lab and try it for yourself.

175
00:21:05,730 --> 00:21:09,570
Remember, this video is intended to help you prepare for lab

176
00:21:09,570 --> 00:21:13,250
by providing a demonstration of the proper experimental technique.

177
00:21:13,890 --> 00:21:19,170
It is not intended as a replacement for reading your lab manual or the supplementary material.

178
00:21:19,890 --> 00:21:22,290
In order to become a great experimentalist,

179
00:21:22,290 --> 00:21:25,570
it is important that you understand both theory and technique.

180
00:21:26,210 --> 00:21:28,210
Now it's your turn. Good luck!
